<div class="meta">Drill 0019 · pairs with lesson 0019</div>
<h1>Drill: Distance Fog in a Forward Renderer</h1>

<div class="q"><h3>1. Neon grass</h3>
<p>After assigning the fog shader to every model material, buildings haze
but the grass plane stays fully saturated to the horizon. Why, and what's
the fix?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p><code>DrawPlane</code>/<code>DrawCube</code> go through rlgl's immediate
batch, which ignores material shaders — it uses the shader set by
<code>BeginShaderMode</code>. Wrap the 3D pass in
<code>BeginShaderMode(fog)</code> / <code>EndShaderMode()</code> so both
paths share the shader.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>2. Why does the same vertex shader work for models AND
batch primitives?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>It computes <code>fragPosition = matModel * vertexPosition</code>. Models
carry their placement in <code>matModel</code>; batch vertices are already
world-space and get <code>matModel = identity</code> — the expression is
correct either way.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>3. Pick the curve</h3>
<p>You want the plaza (40 units) crisp but the city edge (200+) gone. Why
exp² over linear or plain exp?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>exp² is nearly flat close-in (at density 0.006: ~5% at 40 units) then
rolls off steeply (~80% at 220). Linear creates a visible "fog wall" at its
start distance; plain exp dulls even nearby objects.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>4. Fog color choice</h3>
<p>What goes wrong if fogColor is gray instead of the sky clear color?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>Distant geometry fades toward gray while the sky behind it stays blue —
buildings become gray silhouettes pasted on a blue backdrop. Matching the
sky makes far geometry melt into the horizon, which is the entire trick.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>5. Who frees the shader?</h3>
<p>The fog shader is assigned to ~30 model materials. What would double-free
it, and what is raylib's actual contract?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>Calling <code>UnloadMaterial</code> on those materials would free the
shader once per material. But <code>UnloadModel</code> deliberately leaves
material shaders alone (shared-resource contract, per raylib's source
comment) — so Assets assigns freely and unloads the shader exactly once in
its destructor.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>6. GPU skinning caveat</h3>
<p>Why did the custom shader not break character animation here, and when
would it?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>raylib 5.5 skins on the CPU — posed vertices are written into the mesh
before drawing, so any shader that reads <code>vertexPosition</code> sees
the animated pose. Under GPU skinning the vertex shader itself applies bone
matrices; a replacement shader without that code would freeze characters in
bind pose.</p>
</div></details></div>
